    "created_at": "2023-02-06T09:29:05Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-02-06T09:29:05Z",
    "body": "I would say that there is nothing specific at this stage.\r\n\r\nMost of the entries in the indices page, save events and MIME types, are references to things defined elsewhere in the spec. What ends up in webref are the definitions, not the references, so crawler does not do much with underlying tables in the indices page (except for events, see below). All definitions in the HTML spec follow the data definitions model, we don't need to maintain data or parsing rules manually anymore (well, we maintain a [list of obsolete elements](https://github.com/w3c/webref/blob/main/ed/elementspatches/html.json.patch) manually because they're not defined like other elements, but the list hardly ever changes and these elements don't appear in the indices page in any case).\r\n\r\nWe haven't tried to complete the data in `@webref/elements` with information from the [Elements table](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/indices.html#elements-3) yet. Data already maps elements with their interface. We could easily add content attribute based on definitions, no need to add further structure. Categories, context and content model would be harder to extract. But they are also harder to represent in a machine-readable way. I think that we would extract them from the definition of the element itself in any case (e.g. from [the `a` element](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/text-level-semantics.html#the-a-element)).\r\n\r\nFor events, to make things fully machine-readable, what's missing is a common mechanism that authors could use to specify the event interface and target interfaces. The crawler parses the table to extract the info it needs, or looks at the prose that surrounds the event definition when there's no table. That's brittle. Ideally, info would be available through `data-xx` attributes attached to the event's definition. This is not specific to the HTML spec though, and I don't have a concrete proposal to make at this stage.\r\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
